WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.220
YUFENG GUO: Getting
high quality data

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:05.610
is paramount for good
machine learning outcomes.

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.590
But public data
sets aren't always

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:10.570
suitable for custom use cases.

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:12.270
So how can you get
your data labeled

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:16.290
efficiently without spending
days in front of the computer?

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:18.426
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:18.426 --> 00:00:22.314
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:28.360
Welcome to "AI
Adventures," where

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:32.030
we explore the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:34.400
My name is Yufeng Guo,
and on this episode,

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.150
we're going to take a look
at the cloud AI platform

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:39.520
data labeling
service and see how

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.740
it can help you
create labeled data

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:45.580
sets that you can use to
train machine learning models.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:48.820
While you should always try
to collect data in such a way

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.970
that the labels are known
at the time of collection,

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:53.840
that isn't always possible.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:56.470
Especially in the case of,
say, crowdsourced data,

00:00:56.470 --> 00:01:00.070
or unstructured data like
images and video or text,

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:01.930
labeling or data
after collecting it

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:03.920
might be necessary.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.090
The data labeling service lets
you work with human labelers

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:09.250
to create labels for
data that you can then

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:12.130
use to train machine
learning models.

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:15.880
Let's learn a little bit more
about how this system works.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:17.850
Say you've collected
a representative data

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:20.650
set that you want to
classify or analyze,

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:24.520
and you're ready to try out
creating a data labeling task.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:25.660
What does it take?

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:27.190
Let's dive in.

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:30.040
The data labeling service
has three core resources

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:33.750
that it uses to define
a labeling task--

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:37.493
data sets, labels
sets, and instructions.

00:01:37.493 --> 00:01:39.160
You'll need all three
in order to create

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:41.120
a labeling task for your data.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:42.980
Now let's look at
each of these in turn.

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:45.820
The data set resource is,
of course, your data set.

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:49.630
It's represented as a CSV
file on Google Cloud Storage.

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:52.720
Each row of the CSV file
points to an individual element

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:53.720
of data.

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:55.930
For instance, if you
a bunch of images,

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:59.950
each row is a GCS
path to an image file.

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:03.980
For text data, each row should
be a path to a text file.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:05.830
In addition to the
CSV file, you'll

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:07.990
also need to give
the data set a name

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:11.560
and select the data type--
images, video, or text.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.120
Once you've created
your data set,

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:15.010
you'll need to create
your label set.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:19.600
A label set is the set of labels
you want your human labelers

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:21.520
to use to label your images.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:25.870
Each label set can
contain up to 100 labels.

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:28.540
You can mix and match data
sets with different label sets

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:30.760
depending on what model
you're trying to train,

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:32.650
or you might even
use the same label

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:35.830
set for several
different data sets.

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:39.430
Try to avoid using labels
whose meanings might overlap,

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:41.500
as this can confuse labelers.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:43.510
It is also worth
noting that you can

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:47.740
have a label called
neither and both,

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:50.170
which might be useful
especially in, say,

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:51.650
classification scenario.

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:53.750
Once you have a data set
and corresponding label

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:56.800
set created, it's time to
create some instructions

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:58.090
for your labelers.

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:01.030
Here's some tips for
making great instructions.

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:04.210
First up, remember that
labels don't have your domain

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:07.150
knowledge so make sure your
instructions are easy enough

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:08.350
to understand.

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:10.390
Secondly, be sure
to include a list

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:14.830
of all your possible labels with
a description of each label.

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:17.800
And third, in your
instructions, include

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.110
examples for every
single label, making

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:23.350
sure to give at least three
positive examples and at least

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:27.580
one negative example covering
different kinds of cases.

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:30.950
Fourth, clarify as many
edge cases as you can.

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:34.090
For example, with say
bounding boxes and images,

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:36.730
be sure to state whether a
partially covered item should

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:41.860
be included and how tightly that
downing box needs to be drawn.

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:45.460
The system requires that
you submit PDF instructions.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:48.550
So be sure to have some
way to output to PDF.

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:50.570
There is lots of
ways to do this.

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:52.300
One option that
I would recommend

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:55.360
is to just write it up in
Google slides or Google Docs

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:57.670
and save as PDF.

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:00.430
Now that we've created
a data set, label set,

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:03.490
and instructions, we're ready
to bring them all together

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:05.900
to create a labeling task.

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:08.980
The data labeling service
supports many different types

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:10.540
of labeling tasks.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:14.220
For image data, you can do six
different types of labeling.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.019
There is classification,
bounding boxes,

00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:22.270
oriented bounding box, bounding
polygon, polyline tasks,

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:25.030
or segmentation tasks.

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:27.250
For video data, the
data label and service

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:31.960
supports classification, object
detection, object tracking,

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:33.800
and events.

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:36.010
Now, finally, for text
labeling, the service

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:39.970
supports classification,
classification with sentiment,

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:42.460
and finally, entity extraction.

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:45.520
I want to close this video
with some tips for success

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:48.400
and common questions about
the data labeling service.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:51.070
There is always a concern
about how to ensure

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:53.200
high labeling quality.

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.750
One way to ensure the best
labeling quality possible

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:59.020
is to request multiple
human labelers to annotate

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:00.970
each piece of your data.

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:03.580
In cases where there is
disagreement on labeling,

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:05.710
additional opinions
from the other labelers

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:08.080
are gathered until
there is a consensus

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:11.950
or the maximum number of
labelers have been reached.

00:05:11.950 --> 00:05:13.610
Let's take a look at an example.

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:16.120
Say we chose to
have three labels.

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:19.180
For image classification
tasks, all three labelers

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:22.090
will classify each image,
and the majority vote

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:24.160
will decide the final answer.

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:27.350
For image bounding box
tasks, the first labeler

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:31.060
will draw the box and the second
labeler then can verify them.

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:34.060
If the second labeler
disagrees and makes edits,

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:38.140
continue on to the third one
to get a majority opinion.

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:39.730
Another potential
area of questions

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:42.610
is around who will
label my data.

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:45.610
All data sets are labeled
by officially onboarded

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:47.410
subprocessors, and
you can find out

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:49.360
more information
and all the details

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:52.570
at the GCP data processing
and security terms.

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:54.290
Anytime you're
transmitting data,

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:57.470
the security and protection
of that data is paramount.

00:05:57.470 --> 00:05:59.890
It's worth noting
that all data stored

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:03.130
in Google Cloud is already
encrypted by default

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:05.290
both in transit and at rest.

00:06:05.290 --> 00:06:08.830
Human labelers can only view
your data during labeling.

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:11.530
The data labeling service
is also HIPAA compliant,

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:14.450
so for those of you working
with sensitive health care data,

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:17.680
rest assured that it will be
handled in a proper manner.

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:22.510
One final tip, ramp up your data
labeling jobs incrementally.

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:25.310
Start your first labeling job
with a small amount of data

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:27.820
then see whether the
results are what you expect.

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:29.980
You can then revise
your instructions

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:32.080
and then create subsequent
jobs and iterate

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:34.060
until you feel
comfortable sending

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:36.880
larger and larger quantities
of data to be labeled.

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:39.100
This will help you get
high quality results

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:40.840
and make the best
use of your budget,

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:42.740
both in terms of time and money.

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:45.220
I hope this was a useful
overview of the data labeling

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:47.920
service and how you
can get going with it.

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:51.100
And even if you don't need to
use a data labeling service,

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:53.560
these tenants of label
creation and instructions

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:55.530
are important for
creating accurate data

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:58.300
sets for your machine
learning training.

